- Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo, an environmental engineer at the Federal University of Technology Owerri, conducted a 14-month field trial on cadmium-contaminated farmland in southeastern Nigeria.
- She introduced a biochar-mycorrhizal fungus combination that reduced cadmium concentrations in the topsoil by 58 percent, well above the 30 percent threshold regulators consider significant.
- Crop yields on the treated plots increased by 41 percent compared to untreated adjacent plots.
- The trial site had laterite-rich soil with a pH between 5.2 and 5.7; Okonkwo has noted that the biochar-fungus interaction may behave differently in alkaline or calcium-rich soils common in other agricultural regions.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo's biochar-mycorrhizal treatment reduced cadmium levels by 58 percent and raised crop yields by 41 percent in a 14-month trial on Nigerian farmland, results that surpass regulatory thresholds and demonstrate that the technique is ready for widespread agricultural adoption."},{"key":"B","text":"In a 14-month trial on cadmium-contaminated farmland, Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo found that a biochar-mycorrhizal combination reduced cadmium by 58 percent and boosted crop yields by 41 percent, though she cautions that the laterite-rich, mildly acidic soil at her trial site may have uniquely favored the treatment, and results could differ in alkaline or calcium-rich soils."},{"key":"C","text":"Dr. Okonkwo has noted that her biochar-mycorrhizal fungus treatment, tested on laterite-rich soil with a pH between 5.2 and 5.7, may not work in alkaline or calcium-rich soils, a significant limitation that calls into question whether the approach merits further investment."},{"key":"D","text":"Environmental engineer Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo of the Federal University of Technology Owerri conducted a 14-month field trial in southeastern Nigeria using a biochar-mycorrhizal fungus combination on cadmium-contaminated farmland with laterite-rich soil."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal requires two moves: framing the findings as promising and cautioning about generalizability. The option beginning with "In a 14-month trial on cadmium-contaminated farmland" achieves both by citing the strong results (58 percent cadmium reduction, 41 percent yield increase) and then explicitly noting the soil-specificity caveat. The option beginning with "Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo''s biochar-mycorrhizal treatment" presents the results but declares the technique ready for widespread adoption, an unsupported claim that contradicts the cautionary half of the goal. The option beginning with "Dr. Okonkwo has noted" foregrounds the limitation and adds a dismissive editorial judgment ("calls into question whether the approach merits further investment") not found in the notes, failing to present the findings as promising. The option beginning with "Environmental engineer Dr. Adaeze Okonkwo" describes the study setup without reporting any results or limitations, fulfilling neither part of the rhetorical goal.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Text 2: Archaeologist Martin Delvaux examined 74 excavated burial sites in Normandy containing objects of Scandinavian origin and found that gift items were frequently interred alongside recipients of markedly different social standings, from minor landholders to regional lords. Delvaux argues that these patterns indicate gift exchange functioned as a network-building strategy designed to create lateral alliances across social strata rather than to reinforce vertical hierarchies. The geographic distribution of shared artifact styles, he notes, maps onto known trade corridors rather than onto chains of political subordination.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Sigurdardottir views gift exchange as reinforcing vertical power structures between individuals of unequal rank, whereas Delvaux views it as forging horizontal connections across diverse social positions"},{"key":"B","text":"Sigurdardottir believes gift exchange was confined to the warrior elite, whereas Delvaux argues it extended to all members of Norse society including enslaved laborers"},{"key":"C","text":"Sigurdardottir interprets gifts as having primarily economic value in trade networks, whereas Delvaux interprets them as having primarily symbolic value in religious ceremonies"},{"key":"D","text":"Sigurdardottir argues that gift recipients always accepted their subordinate status willingly, whereas Delvaux argues that recipients routinely refused gifts to preserve their independence"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Sigurdardottir frames gift exchange as encoding hierarchical relationships in which the giver asserts dominance and the recipient acknowledges subordination, a vertical power model. Delvaux''s burial evidence shows gifts distributed across different social strata along trade corridors, which he interprets as lateral alliance-building rather than hierarchy reinforcement. The option about Sigurdardottir confining exchange to the warrior elite overstates her claim; she discusses chieftains but does not exclude other participants, and Delvaux''s text mentions landholders and lords, not enslaved laborers. The option about economic versus religious interpretations mischaracterizes both researchers: Sigurdardottir discusses social rank, not trade economics, and Delvaux discusses alliance networks along trade corridors, not religious ceremonies. The option about recipients always accepting subordination willingly contradicts Sigurdardottir''s own text, which explicitly states that refusing a gift was a means of contesting hierarchy, and Delvaux does not discuss gift refusal at all.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Dr. Kofi Mensah, a developmental psychologist at the University of Toronto, followed 214 children aged 6 to 12 over a four-year period beginning in 2019.
- Half the children received weekly 45-minute violin lessons; the other half participated in a visual arts program of equal duration.
- By the end of the study, the violin group scored 23 percent higher on standardized tests of working memory and 17 percent higher on auditory processing tasks than the visual arts group.
- Both groups showed similar improvements in creative problem-solving, suggesting that general arts engagement, not just music, may support certain cognitive skills.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Dr. Kofi Mensah's four-year study found that children who received violin lessons and children in a visual arts program both improved in creative problem-solving, suggesting that various forms of arts education can benefit cognitive development."},{"key":"B","text":"In a four-year study of 214 children, Dr. Kofi Mensah found that those receiving weekly violin instruction scored 23 percent higher on working memory tests and 17 percent higher on auditory processing tasks than peers in a visual arts program of equal duration, providing measurable evidence that instrumental training yields distinct cognitive advantages."},{"key":"C","text":"Dr. Kofi Mensah, a developmental psychologist at the University of Toronto, conducted a four-year longitudinal study beginning in 2019 that compared the cognitive effects of violin lessons with those of a visual arts program among 214 children aged 6 to 12."},{"key":"D","text":"A longitudinal study by Dr. Kofi Mensah tracked 214 children over four years and divided them into a violin group and a visual arts group, each receiving 45-minute weekly sessions throughout the study period."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to counter the claim that instrumental training has no cognitive benefit. The option beginning with "In a four-year study of 214 children" directly presents the specific cognitive advantages measured in violin students over arts-program peers, supplying the evidence needed to refute the no-benefit claim. The option beginning with "Dr. Kofi Mensah''s four-year study" highlights the shared improvement in creative problem-solving, which supports arts education generally but does not isolate a benefit unique to instrumental training. The option beginning with "Dr. Kofi Mensah, a developmental psychologist" describes the study''s design without reporting any results, so it neither supports nor counters any claim. The option beginning with "A longitudinal study by Dr. Kofi Mensah" provides logistical details about the study''s structure without mentioning any cognitive outcomes.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







- Dr. Renata Szabo, an environmental chemist at Uppsala University, analyzed 53 freshwater lakes across Sweden between March and November 2023.
- Microplastic particles were detected in every lake sampled, including 17 lakes located in remote, uninhabited regions above the Arctic Circle.
- The average concentration was 4,700 particles per liter of sediment, roughly nine times the level a 2019 European Environment Agency benchmark had predicted for Nordic freshwater systems.
- Polyethylene and polypropylene fragments from packaging materials accounted for 62 percent of all particles identified.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Dr. Renata Szabo of Uppsala University sampled 53 Swedish lakes in 2023 and found that polyethylene and polypropylene fragments from packaging made up 62 percent of the microplastic particles detected."},{"key":"B","text":"In a 2023 survey of 53 Swedish lakes, environmental chemist Dr. Renata Szabo found microplastics at concentrations averaging nine times the level predicted by a 2019 European benchmark, with particles present even in remote Arctic lakes with no nearby human settlement."},{"key":"C","text":"Dr. Renata Szabo, an environmental chemist at Uppsala University, conducted a study of microplastic contamination across 53 freshwater lakes in Sweden over a nine-month period in 2023."},{"key":"D","text":"Between March and November 2023, Dr. Szabo analyzed 53 lakes and determined that polyethylene and polypropylene were the most common types of microplastic particles, a finding consistent with packaging waste being a major source of freshwater contamination."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to highlight the unexpected scale of contamination. The option beginning with "In a 2023 survey of 53 Swedish lakes" achieves this by citing the ninefold exceedance of the predicted benchmark and the presence of particles in remote Arctic lakes, both of which convey how surprisingly widespread the contamination is. The option beginning with "Dr. Renata Szabo of Uppsala University" focuses on the composition of the particles rather than the scale of contamination. The option beginning with "Dr. Renata Szabo, an environmental chemist" merely describes the study''s scope and timeline without reporting any findings at all. The option beginning with "Between March and November 2023" emphasizes the types of plastic found and their likely source, which addresses composition rather than the unexpected extent of the problem.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







Text 2: Economist Tunde Fashola surveyed 260 independent bookshop owners across six Nigerian cities between 2017 and 2023. Fashola found that bookshops in cities with strict commercial rent controls closed at nearly the same rate as those in deregulated markets. The primary factor predicting closure, his data suggest, was not rent burden but competition from online booksellers, which captured an increasing share of the customer base regardless of local zoning frameworks.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It supports Achebe's claim by showing that rent controls alone are insufficient without additional zoning measures"},{"key":"B","text":"It challenges the causal mechanism Achebe identifies by showing that bookshop closures occur at similar rates under different zoning regimes"},{"key":"C","text":"It extends Achebe's argument by demonstrating that multinational chains are even more damaging in cities with rent controls"},{"key":"D","text":"It confirms Achebe's conclusion while substituting online retail for multinational chains as the primary competitive threat to bookshops"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Fashola''s finding that bookshop closure rates were nearly identical in regulated and deregulated markets directly undermines Achebe''s claim that zoning deregulation is the cause of closures, because the outcome she attributes to deregulation also occurs where regulations exist. The option about supporting Achebe by showing rent controls are insufficient misreads the relationship: Fashola''s data does not suggest that additional zoning measures would help, but rather that the entire zoning explanation is secondary to online competition. The option about extending the argument to show chains are more damaging under rent controls introduces a claim Fashola''s research does not make; his data points to online sellers, not chains, as the key factor. The option about confirming Achebe''s conclusion is wrong because Fashola''s finding contradicts rather than confirms the zoning-based explanation, even though both authors discuss bookshop closures.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







Text 2: Pediatric sleep researcher David Kavanagh contends that morning cognitive performance in school-age children is determined primarily by sleep duration and consistency rather than by caloric intake. Kavanagh''s laboratory study of 83 children aged nine to twelve showed that subjects who slept fewer than eight hours exhibited diminished attention regardless of whether they had consumed a full breakfast, while well-rested subjects maintained concentration even when fasting.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By suggesting that the improvements Bains observed may be better explained by sleep patterns than by breakfast consumption"},{"key":"B","text":"By agreeing that breakfast programs enhance concentration and recommending that they be combined with sleep education initiatives"},{"key":"C","text":"By dismissing laboratory research as irrelevant to understanding real-world classroom behavior in elementary schools"},{"key":"D","text":"By proposing that caloric intake and sleep duration contribute equally to morning cognitive performance in children"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Kavanagh''s research indicates that sleep duration, not caloric intake, is the primary determinant of morning concentration, so he would likely argue that the attention improvements Bains attributes to breakfast programs may actually reflect sleep patterns. The option about agreeing and recommending combined initiatives contradicts Kavanagh''s central claim that sleep, not food, drives concentration. The option about dismissing laboratory research as irrelevant is wrong because Kavanagh himself conducts laboratory research; he would not undermine his own methodology. The option about equal contributions misrepresents Kavanagh''s position, which explicitly prioritizes sleep over caloric intake rather than treating them as equivalent factors.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




